From d61fa0eb4d66048d1d40cdc3f512c77318a11c3a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 05:10:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] test(kanban-llm-pacing): a no-reset reading must subtract its
 usage from the sustainable rate [daveey]

Test gap from the post-merge audit of PR #3058 (t_76d6ed98dc6ce8bc; landed 9fe356932d):
in hermes_cli/kanban_llm_pacing.py `_sustainable`, replacing
`max(0.0, cfg.target - used) / WINDOW_SECONDS[kind]` (reading is None or reset_at is None)
with `cfg.target / WINDOW_SECONDS[kind]` survived all pacing tests, because every
reset_at=None fixture used 0% usage. A 40%-used no-reset window then looked like a fresh
window and the pool factor was overestimated. Test-only; no production change.

New tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_llm_pacing_missing_reset.py (9 cases, hand-derived
%/h expected values, not the production formula): five_hour at 0/40/94/95/99% used
(>= target boundary and clamp), a seven_day case, a PACED-vs-OPEN flip with the
admit_limit consequence (8 vs 12), and ordinary-reset / reserved-account controls.

Proof (python 3.14.9 test env, pytest -rs, 0 skipped):
- baseline: new file 9 passed; with the 4 existing pacing files 54 passed.
- mutant A (`cfg.target / WINDOW_SECONDS[kind]`): new file 8 failed / 1 passed (the
  used=0 case, which is the old behaviour); the 4 existing files still 45 passed (the gap).
- mutant B (drop the max(0, ...) clamp): 2 failed (used=99 case, over-target test).
